Patch 18: promote the exhaustive-motion-search threshold to speed 1.

Standalone. Composes with patch 17.

The 101-feature study measured this feature at +7.23% time for +0.12% BD-rate,
ratio 60.3 against a speed-2 bar of 30 -- twice the headroom it needs. It is one
of only three high-ratio speed-2 features that has never been promoted, the
other two having been taken to speed 1 by patches P11 (CCSO early termination,
+22.37%) and P14 (WienerNS refinement, +2.78%), both of which passed.

Why this is deliberately a marginal arm. Promotion has reduced the ratio at
every measurement so far, because BD-rate grows faster than time saved:
transform-statistics pruning fell from 49.0 at speed 4 to 34.0 at speed 3, a
factor of 0.69. Applying that factor here gives 60.3 x 0.69 = 41.6 against a
speed-1 bar of 35. It clears, but not comfortably, and one more degradation
step of the same size would not.

That is the reason to run it. The promotion frontier has paid six times out of
seven and will stop paying somewhere; this arm is close enough to the edge to
locate it. A pass extends the frontier by one more feature, and a failure is the
more valuable result, because it tells us where to stop spending arms on the
mechanism that has otherwise dominated this project.

One structural note. This is a straight assignment move, not a new conditional.
Patch P10 tried a "leaf" variant of this same feature -- adding a per-block gate
-- and came back 1.6-1.7% SLOWER. That is the consistent pattern: across both
patch sets, every patch that ADDED a conditional to a hot path (0012, P09, P10,
P12, and 0016's removal set) failed to deliver speed and four were outright
slower, while every patch that MOVED an existing assignment to a slower preset
delivered. Assignment moves cost nothing at runtime; conditionals are evaluated
on every block whether or not they fire.

-DAVM_S1_EXH_MV_THRESH=0 disables the promotion without a patch edit.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/speed_features.c b/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
index f363519..262f728 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/speed_features.c
@@ -339,6 +339,38 @@ static void set_good_speed_features_framesize_independent(
   sf->rd_sf.perform_coeff_opt = 1;
 
   if (speed >= 1) {
+    // Promote the exhaustive-motion-search threshold to speed 1.
+    //
+    // The 101-feature study measured this as one of the most efficient features
+    // in the encoder: +7.23% time for +0.12% BD-rate, ratio 60.3 against a
+    // speed-2 bar of 30 -- twice the required headroom.
+    //
+    // Speed 1's bar is 35. Promotion has reduced the ratio every time it has
+    // been measured (transform-statistics pruning went 49.0 -> 34.0, a factor
+    // of 0.69), so the arithmetic here is genuinely tight: 60.3 x 0.69 = 41.6,
+    // which clears 35 but not by much. This arm is worth running precisely
+    // because it sits near the point where the promotion frontier stops paying,
+    // and knowing where that is matters more than any single result.
+    //
+    // Note this is a straight assignment move, not a new conditional. Patch P10
+    // tried a "leaf" variant of the same feature -- an added per-block gate --
+    // and came back 1.6-1.7% SLOWER. Every gate-adding patch tried on this
+    // project has cost time; every assignment move has been free. This is the
+    // free kind.
+    //
+    // -DAVM_S1_EXH_MV_THRESH=0 disables the promotion without a patch edit.
+#ifndef AVM_S1_EXH_MV_THRESH
+#define AVM_S1_EXH_MV_THRESH 1
+#endif
+#if AVM_S1_EXH_MV_THRESH
+    if (cpi->twopass.fr_content_type == FC_HIGHMOTION ||
+        cpi->is_screen_content_type) {
+      sf->mv_sf.exhaustive_searches_thresh = (1 << 21);
+    } else {
+      sf->mv_sf.exhaustive_searches_thresh = (1 << 26);
+    }
+#endif
+
     sf->flexmv_sf.prune_non_one_pel_mv_using_best_mv_prec = 1;
     sf->inter_sf.selective_ref_frame = 2;
     sf->inter_sf.prune_newmv_modes_using_prior_rd = 1;
